data: {"type":"tool_execution_start","toolCallId":"toolu_01UnUaqoj6kut9tBm4nZpwq5","toolName":"probe","args":{"question":"What is $\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin\\!\\big(x\\cos x\\big)$?","options":[{"label":"$\\cos(x\\cos x)\\cdot(\\cos x - x\\sin x)$","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\cos(x\\cos x)\\cdot(-\\sin x)$","value":"b"},{"label":"$\\cos(x\\cos x)\\cdot(-x\\sin x)$","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\cos(x\\cos x)\\cdot\\cos x$","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The outer layer is $\\sin(u)$, where $u = x\\cos x$. Differentiating it gives $\\cos(x\\cos x)$. The inner function is a **product**, so finding $u'$ needs the product rule:\n$$u' = (x)'\\cos x + x(\\cos x)' = \\cos x - x\\sin x$$\nSo\n$$\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin(x\\cos x) = \\cos(x\\cos x)\\cdot(\\cos x - x\\sin x)$$\n$-\\sin x$ comes from treating the derivative of a product as the product of the derivatives, $1\\cdot(-\\sin x)$. $-x\\sin x$ keeps only one of the two product-rule terms, and $\\cos x$ keeps only the other.","correctAnswer":"a"}}

